FAILURES OF HIERARCHICAL MODELS
Hierarchy is good for dependency.
It breaks down with recurrence, overlap, feedback, process, or self-reference.

WHAT HIERARCHIES DO WELL
- Clarify dependency
- Separate meanings
- Support local reasoning
“A hierarchy organizes what depends on what.”

Layer 1 -> Layer 2 -> Layer 3 -> Layer 4
(The Stack / Hierarchical Model)

A. CYCLE / RECURRENCE
A stack assumes linear order.
Many systems loop back on themselves.
Example: Five Phases
  A --> B --> C --> A

B. FEEDBACK / CIRCULAR CAUSATION
Effects become causes.
Behavior changes the conditions that produced it.
Example: Cybernetics
  Output --> Interpretation --> Action --> Output

C. OVERLAP / MULTIPLE BELONGING
Real systems often belong to several wholes at once.
Example: A City Is Not a Tree
  A ∩ B ∩ C  (overlapping sets)

D. MUTUAL CONSTITUTION
Parts and wholes help define each other.
Identity is relational.
Example: Indra's Net
  Fully connected graph of nodes

E. PROCESS / BECOMING
Stable things may be patterns of events,
not fundamental substances.
Example: Whitehead
  t0 --> t1 --> t2 --> t3 --> ...

F. SELF-REFERENCE / STRANGE LOOPS
A system can model itself,
and that model can change the system.
Example: Hofstadter
  Model of Self  <-->  System

WHAT THE STACK CAN’T SAY
cycle | feedback | overlap | co-constitution | becoming | self-reference

BETTER ALTERNATIVE: MULTIPLE GEOMETRIES
Use different structures for different aspects of reality.

Layers          : dependency
Graphs          : relation
Events          : history
Loops           : regulation
Semi-lattices   : participation
Views           : cross-cutting concerns

“Different geometries for different aspects of reality.”

```
STACK / LAYERED HIERARCHY
Type: Ordered linear containment
Variables: layer[n], depends_on(layer[n+1])
Fundamentals: strict top-down dependency, no cycles, local reasoning

Layer 4
  ↑
Layer 3
  ↑
Layer 2
  ↑
Layer 1
```

```
TREE / ORG CHART HIERARCHY
Type: Rooted acyclic directed tree
Variables: node, parent, children[], depth
Fundamentals: single parent, unique path to root, cascade of authority

                CEO
               /   \
            VP-A    VP-B
           /  \       \
        Dir1  Dir2    Dir3
       /  \
     Emp1 Emp2
```

```
TAXONOMY / CLASSIFICATION HIERARCHY
Type: Nested categorical partition
Variables: taxon, rank, parent_taxon, members[]
Fundamentals: mutually exclusive at each level, inheritance of properties

Kingdom
 └── Phylum
      └── Class
           └── Order
                └── Family
                     └── Genus
                          └── Species
```

```
INHERITANCE / CLASS HIERARCHY (OOP)
Type: Is-a directed acyclic graph (usually tree)
Variables: class, superclass, subclasses[], inherited_methods
Fundamentals: specialization downward, polymorphism, single or multiple inheritance

        Animal
       /      \
   Mammal    Bird
    /  \       \
 Dog   Cat    Eagle
```

```
FILESYSTEM / CONTAINMENT HIERARCHY
Type: Nested directory tree
Variables: path, parent_dir, children[], inode
Fundamentals: unique absolute path, containment, no cycles

/
├── home
│   └── user
│       ├── docs
│       └── src
└── etc
    └── config
```

```
CHAIN OF COMMAND / LINEAR AUTHORITY
Type: Totally ordered list with ranks
Variables: rank, superior, subordinates[], command_flow
Fundamentals: transitive authority, no lateral equality at same level

General
  ↓
Colonel
  ↓
Major
  ↓
Captain
  ↓
Lieutenant
```

```
NESTED SETS / CONTAINMENT HIERARCHY
Type: Interval or nested interval encoding
Variables: node, left, right, parent
Fundamentals: descendant ranges lie inside ancestor range

[Root 1-20]
  [A 2-9]
    [A1 3-4]
    [A2 5-8]
  [B 10-19]
    [B1 11-14]
    [B2 15-18]
```
